t to drive forward um innovation and  deployment in areas like offshore wind  electric vehicles you know it's not fair  to say the government's doing nothing on  this front  but that particular budget felt like it  could have been a lot bolder  particularly when also we need economic  stimulus you know we were still coming  out at the back end of the the lockdowns  then the pandemic impact that we've had  has still been  severe on the economy um as a whole  and there is an opportunity here to  build back greener as the prime minister  had it  um before he started slightly dropped  that phrase in in more recent times um  and and that yeah yeah that budget felt  like a moment when you really could be  more ambitious and start to drive  forward some of these investments we  need to see in areas like energy  efficiency hydrogen carbon capture and  storage smart grids  and various other areas so uh yeah i  think a missed opportunity from this the  wws conclusion i think a lot of people  will agree i'll pick your brains on  renewables in a second uh james but judy  we saw those big climate commitments  didn't we  after cop 26. um does some of that  package that was announced by the  chancellor undermine that the wwf is  saying 55 billion pounds of that could  drive up emissions  it is really important for us to give an  image of absolute ambitious support for  the movements into the future because  after a lot of people don't realize it  but the cop 26 presidency is ongoing  until cop 27 is in place we are in one  of the big driving seats for actions or  climate emergency so we need to be  ambitious seem to put massive amounts of  needed money in that direction and be an  example for others to lead the way as  james says it's not that we don't do  things that are  and in a way quite important for example  the green jobs initiative where the  government is investing in green jobs  and hopes to put in another 2 million  jobs by 2030. that's a good initiative  but it's the overall ambition that has  to come out right for climate emergency  there is that targeted investment from  the government though james isn't there  you mentioned it earlier in the form of  wind energy but there is this huge  support for renewables that is coming  pretty quickly isn't it  yeah i mean the government's done a lot  on on offshore wind in particular and it  was around that time they announced the  next wave of funding to support the next  wave of offshore wind farms recently  we've seen  scottish um the the scottish crown  estate announcing that it's gonna lease  more of the seabed for offshore win so  you know there's been huge progress on  that front and that will continue uh and  also we're starting to see sort of  onshore renewables come back online as  well so we are seeing that progress what  is sometimes missed about this though is  is the sheer scale of what's required is  much larger than many people  anticipate or understand if we are to  fully decarbonize and get gas and coal  off the grid as completely pretty much  unabated gas and coal off the grid  completely within the space of only  about 15 years  then you just need renewables at truly  massive scale and you need them as  quickly as possible and the economic  arguments for them have fallen away  because they're increasingly cost  competitive i mean you know there are  cheaper than gas in many ways on a  levelized basis currently and we're also  seeing the cost of energy storage  systems fall and and we're starting to  see really exciting work going on in  hydrogen as well where you could start  to produce hydrogen using excess  renewable generation  capacity so  we just it's it's the thing where  everything's pointing towards we need to  be doing this at scale and we need to be  doing it really fast and any potential  barriers that stand in the way whether  that's the government not being as  ambitious as it could be in terms of the  contracts it's providing or planning  constraints or t